  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when your brand new tile floor just doesn't look quite right, right? Often, an uneven tile floor comes down to the surface it was laid on. If the subfloor wasn't properly prepared or leveled before tiling began, those imperfections will show through the finished tile. Think of it like trying to paint a lumpy wall – no matter how good the paint, the lumps are still there! Another common culprit is poor alignment or 'lippage,' which is when one tile edge is higher than the adjacent one. This can happen if the installer isn't meticulous about spacing and keeping things perfectly flat. Weak adhesion from improperly mixed mortar can also cause issues, leading to tiles lifting or feeling loose. These kinds of problems aren't just cosmetic; they can lead to cracking or detachment down the road. It’s why getting a professional involved, especially for large-format tiles or if your subfloor has existing issues, can save you a lot of headaches and money in the long run. They know how to diagnose and fix these underlying problems before they become bigger. If you’re seeing these issues, it might be time to call in someone with experience.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
